(window.DETAIL_C = window.DETAIL_C || {})["mth-12-01"] = {"mth-12-01-m01":{"idea":"A statement is a sentence that is clearly true or false, but not both.","steps":["'The sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°' is true, so it is a statement.","A command or request is not true or false.","A question is not true or false. An open sentence like x + 3 = 5 is true for some x and false for others, so it is not a statement."],"others":"'Please draw a triangle...' is a command. 'Is the sum ... 180°?' is a question. 'x + 3 = 5' is an open sentence.","trap":"Statement means a definite truth value.","answer":"The sum of the angles of a triangle is 180°"},"mth-12-01-m02":{"idea":"'p and q' needs both parts to hold.","steps":["p ∧ q is the conjunction.","It is true only if p is true and q is true.","If even one part is false, the whole statement is false."],"others":"A true p with a false q gives false. Both false gives false. 'Either, but not both' describes exclusive or, not 'and'.","trap":"AND needs both.","answer":"both p and q are true"},"mth-12-01-m03":{"idea":"'p or q' fails only when both parts fail.","steps":["p ∨ q is true if at least one part is true.","The only way to make it false is to make both parts false.","So it is false only for p = F and q = F."],"others":"One true part already makes p ∨ q true. 'Either false but not both' and 'both true' do not give false.","trap":"OR is false only for FF.","answer":"both p and q are false"},"mth-12-01-m04":{"idea":"Each simple statement doubles the number of rows.","steps":["Each simple statement can be T or F.","With 3 statements there are 2 × 2 × 2 = 2³ rows.","2³ = 2 × 2 × 2 = 8 rows."],"others":"6, 3 and 9 are not powers of 2.","trap":"n statements: 2ⁿ rows.","answer":"8"},"mth-12-01-m05":{"idea":"To deny 'all', one exception is enough.","steps":["'All dogs are brown' fails if even one dog is not brown.","So the negation is 'Some dog is not brown'.","It does not need every dog to be non-brown."],"others":"'No dog is brown' is too strong. 'All dogs are black' is also too strong. 'Some dogs are brown' can be true at the same time as the original.","trap":"Negation of 'all' is 'some are not'.","answer":"Some dog is not brown"},"mth-12-01-m06":{"idea":"An 'if p then q' promise is broken only when p happens and q does not.","steps":["p → q is false only for p = T and q = F.","When p is false, the implication is true whatever q is.","In the other three rows it is true."],"others":"p false and q true gives T. Both true gives T. Both false gives T.","trap":"False antecedent makes the implication true.","answer":"p is true and q is false"},"mth-12-01-m07":{"idea":"A pattern true in every row is a tautology.","steps":["Either p is true or ~p is true.","So p ∨ ~p is true in every row.","That makes it a tautology."],"others":"A contingency is true in some rows and false in others. A contradiction is always false. The pattern is always true, so it is not 'neither'.","trap":"p ∨ ~p: always true.","answer":"a tautology"},"mth-12-01-m08":{"idea":"De Morgan: the negation of 'and' is 'or' of the negations.","steps":["p ∧ q fails when at least one part fails.","That is ~p or ~q.","So ~(p ∧ q) = ~p ∨ ~q."],"others":"~p ∧ ~q is the negation of p ∨ q. p ∧ ~q and p ∨ q are not equivalent to the negation of p ∧ q.","trap":"Flip the connective and negate both parts.","answer":"~p ∨ ~q"},"mth-12-01-m09":{"idea":"De Morgan: the negation of 'or' is 'and' of the negations.","steps":["p ∨ q fails only when both parts fail.","That is ~p and ~q together.","So ~(p ∨ q) = ~p ∧ ~q."],"others":"~p ∨ ~q is the negation of p ∧ q. The other forms do not match the truth table.","trap":"Flip the connective and negate both parts.","answer":"~p ∧ ~q"},"mth-12-01-m10":{"idea":"An implication is the same as 'not p or q'.","steps":["p → q is false only when p is true and q is false.","~p ∨ q is false in exactly that row.","So p → q ≡ ~p ∨ q."],"others":"q → p is the converse, a different statement. ~p ∧ q and p ∨ ~q have different truth tables.","trap":"Check the single false row.","answer":"~p ∨ q"},"mth-12-01-m11":{"idea":"The contrapositive reverses the parts and negates both.","steps":["Start with p → q.","Swap the parts and negate each: ~q → ~p.","It is logically equivalent to p → q."],"others":"~q ∧ ~p is a conjunction. ~p → ~q is the inverse. q → p is the converse.","trap":"Contrapositive = equivalent to the original.","answer":"~q → ~p"},"mth-12-01-m12":{"idea":"The inverse negates both parts but keeps the order.","steps":["Start with p → q.","Negate both parts and keep the order: ~p → ~q.","It is equivalent to the converse, not to the original."],"others":"p → ~q negates only one part. ~q → ~p is the contrapositive. ~q ∧ ~p is a conjunction.","trap":"Converse swaps. Inverse negates. Contrapositive does both.","answer":"~p → ~q"},"mth-12-01-m13":{"idea":"Work out each part with the given truth values, then use the biconditional rule.","steps":["p = T and q = F, so p → q is F.","~p is F.","A biconditional is true when both sides have the same value: F ↔ F is T."],"others":"False would need different values on the two sides. 'Both' and 'cannot be decided' do not apply, since the values are given.","trap":"Two false sides make the biconditional true.","answer":"true"},"mth-12-01-m14":{"idea":"A biconditional is true when both sides agree.","steps":["p ↔ q means 'p if and only if q'.","It is true for TT and FF.","It is false for TF and FT."],"others":"Different truth values make it false. 'Both true only' leaves out FF. 'p true whatever q is' ignores q.","trap":"p ↔ q is the same as (p → q) ∧ (q → p).","answer":"p and q have the same truth value"},"mth-12-01-m15":{"idea":"The negation of p → q is p ∧ ~q.","steps":["'If it rains, I stay at home' is p → q.","The promise fails only if it rains and I do not stay at home.","That is p ∧ ~q."],"others":"'Does not rain or I stay' is the original in another form (~p ∨ q). The others use the wrong connective or the wrong signs.","trap":"The negation of an implication is a conjunction.","answer":"It rains and I do not stay at home"},"mth-12-01-m16":{"idea":"Negating 'for every' gives 'there exists', with the property negated.","steps":["The statement is 'for every x, x² ≥ 0'.","Its negation is 'there is an x with x² < 0'.","The original is true, so its negation is false."],"others":"'For no real number x is x² less than 0' is the same as the original. 'For every x, x² < 0' is too strong. 'There is an x with x² ≥ 0' does not deny the original.","trap":"Flip the quantifier and negate the property.","answer":"there is a real number x such that x² is less than 0"},"mth-12-01-m17":{"idea":"Switches in series work like AND.","steps":["Current flows through a series connection only when both switches are closed.","That is the 'and' operation.","So series corresponds to p ∧ q."],"others":"Parallel switches give p ∨ q. ~p ∨ ~q and p → q are not series circuits.","trap":"Series = and. Parallel = or.","answer":"p ∧ q"},"mth-12-01-m18":{"idea":"Switches in parallel work like OR.","steps":["The current has two paths.","It flows if either path is closed.","So the condition is p ∨ q: at least one switch is closed."],"others":"Exactly one open is only a part of the cases. Both open means no current. Both closed is only one of the cases.","trap":"Parallel needs at least one closed.","answer":"at least one of the switches is closed"},"mth-12-01-m19":{"idea":"The dual swaps ∧ and ∨ and leaves the letters alone.","steps":["Start with p ∧ (q ∨ r).","Interchange ∧ and ∨.","The dual is p ∨ (q ∧ r)."],"others":"Keeping both connectives, or negating the letters, does not give the dual.","trap":"Do not negate the simple statements.","answer":"p ∨ (q ∧ r)"},"mth-12-01-m20":{"idea":"To form the dual also swap t and c.","steps":["Interchange ∨ with ∧.","Interchange t (always true) with c (always false).","(p ∨ ~q) ∧ t becomes (p ∧ ~q) ∨ c."],"others":"Keeping t does not swap. Negating the letters changes the statement. Keeping the ∨ in the first bracket does not interchange.","trap":"Letters keep their signs.","answer":"(p ∧ ~q) ∨ c"},"mth-12-01-m21":{"idea":"A switch and its complement in parallel always give a path.","steps":["Exactly one of S and S′ is closed at any time.","So the current always has a path.","This is the circuit for S ∨ S′, true in every row, like a tautology."],"others":"Never flowing would be a contradiction, which is S and S′ in series. The other options cover only half of the cases.","trap":"S ∨ S′ is always true.","answer":"always allows current to flow, like a tautology"},"mth-12-01-m22":{"idea":"p → q is true in some rows and false in one, so it is a contingency.","steps":["It is false only for p = T and q = F.","It is true in the other three rows.","A pattern that is sometimes true and sometimes false is a contingency."],"others":"It is not always equal to its converse. It is not a tautology, since there is one false row. It is not a contradiction.","trap":"One false row means it is not a tautology.","answer":"a contingency"},"mth-12-01-m23":{"idea":"Check whether all the conditions can be true together.","steps":["To be true, we need p true.","We also need p → q true, so q must be true.","But we also need ~q true, which contradicts q being true.","So the pattern is false in every row: a contradiction."],"others":"A contingency or tautology would need at least one true row. Containing p → q does not make it equivalent to p → q.","trap":"Look for a clash between parts.","answer":"a contradiction"},"mth-12-01-m24":{"idea":"The negation of an implication is p and not q.","steps":["p → q ≡ ~p ∨ q.","By De Morgan, ~(~p ∨ q) = p ∧ ~q.","It is true only for p = T and q = F."],"others":"~p ∨ q is the implication itself. ~p ∧ q and p ∨ ~q have other truth tables.","trap":"The negation of an implication is a conjunction.","answer":"p ∧ ~q"},"mth-12-01-m25":{"idea":"The negation of a biconditional is true when the two sides differ.","steps":["p ↔ q is true when p and q agree.","So its negation is true when they differ.","That means p true and q false, or q true and p false: (p ∧ ~q) ∨ (q ∧ ~p)."],"others":"The other options are all equivalent to p ↔ q itself, not its negation.","trap":"Negation of ↔ is exclusive or.","answer":"(p ∧ ~q) ∨ (q ∧ ~p)"},"mth-12-01-m26":{"idea":"Rewrite the implications with 'not' and 'or', then simplify.","steps":["q → p is ~q ∨ p.","So p → (q → p) becomes ~p ∨ (~q ∨ p).","~p ∨ p is always true, so the whole statement is true in every row."],"others":"It is not equivalent to q. It is not a contradiction. It is not a contingency, since it is never false.","trap":"Look for p ∨ ~p inside.","answer":"a tautology"},"mth-12-01-n01":{"idea":"A table with n simple statements has 2ⁿ rows.","steps":["Each of the 5 statements can be T or F.","Number of rows = 2⁵ = 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 × 2 = 32."],"trap":"Powers of two double each time.","answer":"32"},"mth-12-01-n02":{"idea":"Count the rows where (p ∧ q) ∨ r is true.","steps":["It is true whenever r is true: 4 of the 8 rows.","If r is false, we need p ∧ q, which is one row (p = T, q = T, r = F).","Total = 4 + 1 = 5."],"trap":"Do not count a row twice.","answer":"5"},"mth-12-01-n03":{"idea":"Check every one of the 8 rows for (p → q) ∧ (q → r).","steps":["Both implications must hold.","The rows TTT, FTT, FFT and FFF satisfy both.","In the other rows, either p is true with q false, or q is true with r false.","So 4 rows are true."],"trap":"Chain: p gives q, q gives r.","answer":"4"},"mth-12-01-n04":{"idea":"Count the rows where (p ↔ q) ∨ r is true.","steps":["If r is true, all 4 such rows are true.","If r is false, we need p ↔ q, which holds for TT and FF: 2 rows.","Total = 4 + 2 = 6."],"trap":"p ↔ q is true when p and q agree.","answer":"6"},"mth-12-01-n05":{"idea":"An implication is true when its first part is false or its second part is true.","steps":["p ∨ q is false when both p and q are false: 2 rows.","p ∧ r is true when both p and r are true: 2 rows (q free).","These two sets of rows do not overlap, so the total is 2 + 2 = 4."],"trap":"Check that the two sets do not overlap.","answer":"4"},"mth-12-01-n06":{"idea":"Use inclusion and exclusion on the two conditions.","steps":["p ∧ q is true in 4 of the 16 rows (r and s are free).","r ∧ s is true in 4 rows.","Both are true in 1 row, which was counted twice.","Total = 4 + 4 − 1 = 7."],"trap":"Subtract the overlap.","answer":"7"},"mth-12-01-n07":{"idea":"Evaluate each statement for p = T, q = F, r = T.","steps":["p ∧ q = F, p ∨ q = T, p → q = F.","q → p = T (the first part is false), p ↔ q = F.","~p ∨ r = T because r is T.","Three statements are true."],"trap":"A false antecedent gives a true implication.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-01-n08":{"idea":"Three implications in a cycle force all three letters to agree.","steps":["p gives q, q gives r, r gives p.","If one letter is true, they are all true.","If one is false, they are all false.","So only TTT and FFF work: 2 rows."],"trap":"A cycle of implications means equivalence.","answer":"2"},"mth-12-01-n09":{"idea":"Translate the circuit to logic, then count the settings.","steps":["The circuit is p ∧ (q ∨ r).","We need p closed: 1 way.","We need at least one of q and r closed: 3 of the 4 ways.","Total = 1 × 3 = 3 settings."],"trap":"q ∨ r has 3 conducting settings out of 4.","answer":"3"},"mth-12-01-n10":{"idea":"Two parallel pairs in series give (p ∨ q) ∧ (r ∨ s).","steps":["Each pair conducts in 3 of its 4 settings.","The two pairs are independent.","Total = 3 × 3 = 9 settings out of 16."],"trap":"Multiply the counts of independent parts.","answer":"9"}};
